<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dordrecht</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dort</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dordrecht is a city in South Holland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, where several branches of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Maas</a> delta meet. Granted town rights in 1220, it is the oldest chartered town of Holland and long held staple rights over river trade. The first free assembly of the States of Holland met there in 1572 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-revolt">Dutch Revolt</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/synod-of-dort">Synod of Dort</a> of 1618–1619 defined Reformed orthodoxy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dordrecht.jpg" alt="Dordrecht" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ben Bender · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Augustijnenkamp,_3311_Dordrecht,_Netherlands_-_panoramio_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The municipality covers the whole of Dordrecht Island, bordered by the rivers Oude Maas, Beneden Merwede, Nieuwe Merwede, Hollands Diep and Dordtsche Kil. The city formed along the Thure river in the midst of peat marshes, in the Rhine-Meuse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheldt">Scheldt</a> delta. The name appears as Thuredriht around 1120 and seems to mean a thoroughfare: a canal or river through which ships were pulled by rope from one river to another. An older explanation from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> trajectum, a ford, was rejected in 1996. English once called it Dort or Dordt, and its inhabitants call it Dordt.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A remark of about 1120 records that Count Dirk IV of Holland had been murdered in 1049 near Thuredrech, which refers to the place. In 1572, four years into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-revolt">Dutch Revolt</a>, representatives of all the cities of Holland except <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>, together with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-beggars">Watergeuzen</a> under William II de la Marck, met in Dordrecht in a secret First Assembly of the Free States. The meeting was a rebellious act, since only King Philip II or his stadtholder, then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fernando-alvarez-de-toledo-3rd-duke-of-alba">Duke of Alba</a>, could call the States of Holland.</p><p>From 13 November 1618 to 9 May 1619 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/synod-of-dort">Synod of Dordrecht</a> met to settle the dispute between the central tenets of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calvinism">Calvinism</a> and the school of thought of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacobus-arminius">Jacobus Arminius</a>, whose followers, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/remonstrants">Remonstrants</a>, had set out their dissent in the Five Articles of Remonstrance of 1610. They were opposed by the Contra-Remonstrants, or Gomarists, led by the theologian Franciscus Gomarus.</p><p>Merchants of Dordrecht helped found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> plantations in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-indies">West Indies</a>, which led at the end of the 17th century to a sugar refining industry; the city had 16 refineries in the 18th century, against 120 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> and 40 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rotterdam">Rotterdam</a>. Its economic importance nonetheless began to wane in that century. From 1780 to 1787 it was a home of the Patriots, who sought to remove the hereditary stadtholder, until it capitulated to Prussian troops on 18 September 1787.</p><p>The opening of the Voorne Canal in 1829 and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nieuwe-waterweg">Nieuwe Waterweg</a> in 1883 ended Dordrecht's role as a first-rate sea harbour; by 1910 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rotterdam">Rotterdam</a> sent about 200 times as much cargo up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Dordrecht is the fifth-largest city of South Holland, with about 123,000 inhabitants in 2025, and the most important city of the Drechtsteden. Its medieval centre has over 950 monuments, seven historic churches and six museums. The Grote Kerk, built between 1285 and 1470, has a 65-metre tower with a carillon of 67 bells. The Nieuwkerk of 1175 is the city's oldest building, and the Augustinian monastery Het Hof, built in 1275, was the meeting place of the First Assembly of the Free States.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dordrecht” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The counts drained peat bogs, built dykes and founded towns such as Haarlem, Delft, Leiden and <b>Dordrecht</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-holland">County of Holland</a></li></ul></section>
